A||N||7||general||Temperature guage||||5||
Z||000000||general||04-20-2000||03:50 PM||||Gentlemen,I have an '86 SE that has just recently been rebuilt. I have been overcoming one hurdle after another trying to get this bad boy back on the road.The main obstacle now is the temperature guage.I have replaced the sending unit on the head of my 2.8 lit.V-6,yet the guage continues to read cold even after the engine warms up.I have too much time and effort in this car to take a chance on running it on the road without knowing just how hot it is.Could the problem be a short in the wiring or can something else affect the circuit?   general||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||04-20-2000||04:32 PM||||Quick way to check wiring- ground both wires from the sender -the gage should peg and the idiot light should light (key on of course)||||reg||1
Z||000002||daved||04-20-2000||06:47 PM||||You do know that this sensor is the one by the distributor?  It has 2 wires, and the plug is usually melted!  I broke the plastic plug part away, cleaned the connectors, and just plugged them into the sensor.  Its worked just fine.||||reg||1
Z||000003||general||04-22-2000||09:20 PM||||   I tried Phil's suggestion and the needle pegged just like he said.I also tried daved's idea and there was no change.I even tried switching the terminals but it did not help.According to the manual ,when the sending unit warms up,the resistance should read 55 ohm's @ 260 deg.F.It would stand to reason that even at 200 deg.F there should be some measurable resistance showing,yet I am reading nothing.Could it be that the parts house may have sold me either a bad sending unit or the wrong one? Any suggestions would be appreciated! general ||||reg||9
Z||000004||Oreif||04-22-2000||09:24 PM||||I ordered a temp sensor from wrenchhead.com and I got the wrong sensor. According to what I found out the sensor I got won't work on a V-6. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||ka4nkf||04-22-2000||09:28 PM||||If the needle peged when you shorted the two wires, then I would bet that you got a bad sensor. also dont use any teflon tape on the threads. use pipe dope.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||04-22-2000||10:01 PM||||The shell of the sensor should be grounded||||reg||1
Z||000007||ggarsk||04-23-2000||04:22 AM||||If it's anything like mine,it could be that the needle is off. Mine shows it cold until I sit in line,then it moves up. The fan kicks on when the guage reads about 130.||||reg||1
